      "text": "Let's define the general expression for each term in the sequence. For a number \\( n \\) from 1 to 1000, the \\( n \\)-th term of the sequence is:\n\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\n\\]\n\nWe want to determine the number of distinct values this expression can take when \\( n \\) cycles through the numbers 1 to 1000.\n\nTo achieve this, let's consider the fraction:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n^2}{1000}\n\\]\n\nAs \\( n \\) varies from 1 to 1000:\n\n- For \\( n = 1 \\),\n  \\[\n  \\frac{1^2}{1000} = \\frac{1}{1000}\n  \\]\n- For \\( n = 1000 \\),\n  \\[\n  \\frac{1000^2}{1000} = 1000\n  \\]\n\nWhen \\( n \\) varies successively, we get a continuous and monotonic decrease from \\(\\frac{1}{1000}\\) to 1000. However, in the integer part of \\(\\frac{n^2}{1000}\\) for successive \\( n \\) values, we observe distinct integer values.\n\nLet's calculate a few values to see how distinct they are:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor &= 0, \\\\\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{2^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor &= \\left\\lfloor 0.04 \\right\\rfloor = 0, \\\\\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{3^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor &= \\left\\lfloor 0.09 \\right\\rfloor = 0, \\\\\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{4^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor &= \\left\\lfloor 0.16 \\right\\rfloor = 0, \\\\\n&\\vdots \\\\\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor &= \\left\\lfloor 1000 \\right\\rfloor = 1000 \n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nIt becomes clear in this case that:\n\\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 0\\) and \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 1000\\).\n\nWe count 1000 non-negative integers inclusive from 0 to 1000.\n\nThis number can be confirmed by noting that:\n\n\\[\n0 \\leq \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor \\leq n^2 \\leq 1000000 - 1 \\leq n^2\n\\]\nfor all \\( n, n \\geq 1 \\) and \\( n \\leq 1000 \\).\n\nThus, all possible values from 0 up to and including 1000 are realized. Hence, the number of distinct values:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1001}\n\\]",
