      "text": "The number of distinct numbers in the list is equal to the number of possible values of $\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor$, where $n$ ranges from 1 to 1000.\n\nFor a given positive integer $m$, the value of $\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor$ can reach either $m$ or $m+1$. So, each possible value of $m$ can correspond to either 2 or 1 distinct number in the list, depending on whether or not it is divisible by 1000.\n\nThe number of special numbers longer than 4,000 cannot be divisible by $1000$.\n\nTherefore, there are $\\boxed{600}$ numbers that can reach $m+1$, and only $\\boxed{400}$ numbers that can reach $m$. The sum of 600 and 400 is $\\boxed{1000}$.\n\nThe answer is: 1000",
