      "text": "To find the number of distinct numbers in the list \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{3^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\dots, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\), we need to analyze the floor function \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) for \\(n\\) ranging from 1 to 1000.\n\nFirst, we note that \\(\\frac{n^2}{1000} = \\frac{n^2}{10^3}\\). For \\(1 \\leq n \\leq 1000\\), \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) ranges from 1 to 999. This is because:\n\n- When \\(n = 1\\), \\(\\frac{1^2}{1000} = \\frac{1}{1000} = 0.001\\) and \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 0\\). So, \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 0\\).\n- When \\(n = 100\\), \\(\\frac{100^2}{1000} = \\frac{10000}{1000} = 10\\) and \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{10000}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 10\\).\n- When \\(n = 999\\), \\(\\frac{999^2}{1000} = \\frac{998001}{1000} = 998.001\\) and \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{998001}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 998\\).\n- When \\(n = 1000\\), \\(\\frac{1000^2}{1000} = \\frac{1000000}{1000} = 1000\\) and \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000000}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 1000\\).\n\nHowever, \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) can only take integer values from 0 to 999, so we need to find how many distinct integer values it can obtain. Since \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) is a continuous function, for each integer \\(k\\) from 0 to 999, there will be at least one \\(n\\) in the range 1 to 1000 such that \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = k\\). But we need to check if these values are distinct.\n\nTo do this, we can think about the function \\(f(n) = \\frac{n^2}{1000}\\) and its square root, \\(g(n) = \\sqrt{n^2} = n\\). The function \\(f(n)\\) is a continuous and increasing function, and it will take on every integer value between 0 and 999 at some integers \\(n\\). Similarly, \\(g(n)\\) is simply the integer part of \\(n^2\\), so it will also take every integer value between 0 and 999 at some integers \\(n\\).\n\nSince \\(f(n)\\) and \\(g(n)\\) are essentially the same function, they will produce the same set of integer values. Therefore, the number of distinct values that \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) can take is 999.\n\nThus, the number of distinct numbers in the list is \\(\\boxed{999}\\).",
